When individual superstructures interact, they form Compounds—complex, multi-nodal alliances that operate under specific, phase-locked rules. Just as chemical formulas dictate the exact ratio of atoms in a molecule, compound formulas in the NAB regime dictate the structural integrity and operational limits of these combined groups.
THE MECHANICS OF COMBINATION
When superstructures combine, they do not simply merge into a single, larger entity. Instead, they maintain their individual identities while sharing specific resources (electrons) to achieve mutual stability.
	•	The Formula as a Contract: A compound formula (such as H_2O or NaCl) acts as a strict operational contract. It defines exactly how many of each superstructure type are required to maintain a stable, refusal-bound state.
	•	The Dominance of Electronegativity: The structure of the compound is entirely dictated by the differing electronegativities of its members.
	•	Ionic Compounds (The Takeover): When a highly aggressive recruiter (like Fluorine) meets a high-turnover wanderer (like Sodium), the wanderer permanently hands over a member. They remain bound together not by sharing, but by the opposing charges of their now-altered superstructures.
	•	Covalent Compounds (The True Alliance): When two similar superstructures (like two Carbons) meet, they share resources equally. This forms a highly stable, interconnected network where neither side dominates.
